## 1. Introduction
Porous materials, such as inorganic zeolites,[1-4] metalorganic frameworks [5,6] or coordination networks[7,8] and covalent organic frameworks,[9-12] find widespread applications in heterogeneous catalysis, adsorption, ion exchange processes.[13- 24] In recent years, there has been a trend of constructing supramolecular organic frameworks with framework structural characteristics based on weak intermolecular interactions, including hydrogen bonds, ion−dipole interactions, C-H∙∙∙π interactions, and/or π∙∙∙π interactions.[25,26] Because the driving force of their synthetic process stems from weak supramolecular interactions, supramolecular organic frameworks are generally easy to synthesize and show good reversibility. However, it is precisely because of their weak supramolecular interactions that supramolecular organic frameworks usually lack sufficient strength and stability. Using a selection of organic building blocks with rigid structures to construct supramolecular organic frameworks could be an effective method to overcome this shortcoming.[26,27] Cucurbit[n]urils (Q[n]s) may be particularly suitable as such organic building blocks due to their rigid hydrophobic cavity and two identical carbonyl-fringed portals.[28] structures of the representative Q[n]s along with plots of the calculated electrostatic potential of the surfaces of the Q[n]s. The electrostatic potentials show that: i) their two open portals are electrostatic potential negative due to being rimmed with carbonyl oxygen atoms; ii) their inner cavity surfaces are almost electrostatic potential neutral, and iii) their outer surfaces are electrostatic potential positive (Figure 1 bottom).
In general, cucurbit[n]urils (Q[n]s) are cage compounds formed by 2n methylene uints bridging n glycoluril moieties, with 2n carbonyl groups distributed around the two open portals, respectively. Generally, the value of the “n” determines the sizes of the cavity and portals of a $\mathsf Q[\boldsymbol{n}]$ . At present, the members of the $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{n}]$ family range from the smallest $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{n}]$ analogues $\mathsf{Q}[4]\mathsf{s}^{[29,30]}$ to the largest Q[15], which has a twisted structure,[31] unsubstituted $\mathsf{Q}[n]\mathsf{s}^{[32-40]}$ and a variety of fully and partially alkyl-substituted Q[n]s,[41-53] In total, there are over a hundred cucurbit[n]urils and analogues known at present time.[28] Figure 1(top) shows the
Therefore, the use of Q[n]s as the building blocks can provide a variety of Q[n]-based supramolecular frameworks (termed ‘‘QSF’’s hereafter). By virtue of their structural characteristics, namely, 1) an electrostatic potential neutral rigid hydrophobic cavity, 2) electrostatic potential negative carbonyl portals, and 3) an electrostatic potential positive outer surface, Q[n]s have provided a focus for development in research aimed at constructing various Q[n]-based QSFs, including Q[n]-based metal-organic frameworks and supramolecular organic frameworks. For example, based on the coordination between the portal carbonyl oxygen atoms and metal ions, a series of Q[n]- based coordination polymers has been reported by our research group and others. A considerable proportion of them have the characteristics of porous frameworks, namely, the so-called Q[n]- based metal-organic frameworks (Figure 2-left);[54-60] there has been a steady trend in constructing Q[n]-based supramolecular organic frameworks via the inclusion effect of the cavity of Q[n]s for specific guests, especially those with aromatic rings at the terminals (Figure 2-right);[26,61-66] Close inspection of these QSFs, including Q[n]-based metal-organic frameworks and supramolecular organic frameworks, reveals that the cavities of Q[n]s are no longer usable because the portals are capped by coordinated metal ions or the cavities are occupied by the included guest molecules.
