## 2. QSFs assembled via the outer surface interaction of Q[n]s
A simpler and more efficient strategy, such as those QSFs is exploiting based on the outer surface interaction of Q[n]s (termed ‘‘OSIQ” hereafter).[67,68] The driving force is derived from the electrostatic potential positive outer surface of Q[n]s. According to the different species interacting with the outer surface of Q[n]s, the QSF construction process can be roughly divided into three categories: self-induced, anion-induced, aromatic ring induced (termed ‘‘aromatic-induced” hereafter). These interactions seem to be extension of the concept of supramolecular multivalency proposed by Reinhoudt and co-workers, who classified the systems with multiple action sites, including multiple coordination sites, multiple host-guest inclusion sites and multiple hydrogen bonds, as supramolecular multivalent systems, the electrostatic potential positive outer surface of $\mathsf Q[\boldsymbol n]$ can provide multiple action sites, including Cmethine-H, Cmethylene-H and so on, and become a powerful and versatile self-assembly pathway suitable for widespread supramolecular systems.[69]
It should be noted that generally, there are large amount of water molecules or protonated water molecules, which play a role of counter ions in Q[n]-based supramolecular frameworks, they can interact with portal carbonyl oxygen atoms of Q[n] molecules through the hydrogen bonding and form a hydrogen bonding network which could be an auxiliary interaction in the formation of Q[n]-based frameworks and supramolecular assemblies.[70] However, when investigating the adsorption properties of $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{\eta}].$ - based frameworks, these water, protonated water molecules (normally in HCl form) and solvent molecules should be removed first by vacuuming to a constant weight at $60-70^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , so the role of them in the frameworks was not considered in this review and they were omitted for clarity in related Figures.
## 2.1. QSFs assembled via the self-induced OSIQ
Thallapally and Tian demonstrated three novel crystalline forms of Q[6] which were prepared by fine control of the mixing process of a hydrochloride solution of Q[6] with ethanol. Two of them (QSFs 4 and 5) were characterized by single crystal X-ray crystallography, their structure is single crystalline, indicating that they have the characteristics of a framework (Figures 5a,c). The self-induced OSIQ between adjacent Q[6] molecules played an important role in the formation such QSFs (Figures 5b,d). Detailed dipole interactions can be compared to the types summarized in Figure 3. There is a large amount of hydrogen bonding between latticed water molecules and portal carbonyl oxygen atoms of Q[6] molecules in these Q[6]-based frameworks, however, they are omitted for clarity in Figure 5. Experimental results showed that both crystalline forms were found to lose crystallinity after exposure to air, indicating the collapse of the Q[6]-based frameworks upon the removal of water or ethanol molecules. Interestingly the third form existing in nanoplate particles exhibited high thermal stability, permanent porosity, and the highest specific surface area of any known solid-state forms of Q[6]. This form is capable of adsorbing $15w t\%$ $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ at $298\mathsf{K}$ and 1 bar, and has one of the highest $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake capacities of reported porous molecular materials. There is a promising future for the use of porous organic molecular materials in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture from flue gas.[75]
When Liu and co-workers investigated the interaction of symmetrical $\mathbf{q},\mathbf{q}^{\prime},\bar{\boldsymbol{\delta}},\bar{\boldsymbol{\delta}}^{\prime}$ -tetramethyl-cucurbit[6]uril (TMeQ[6]) with $\mathsf{C u C l}_{2}$ at different chloride anion concentration conditions they obtained a metal free TMeQ[6]-based supramolecular framework (6). The framework formed in 1.0 M hydrochloric acid solution. Each TMeQ[6] molecule in the framework interacts with four adjacent TMeQ[6] molecules perpendicular to each other via the self-induced OSIQ (Figure 6a). The mutually perpendicular TMeQ[6] molecules can further fuse forming 2D porous networks (Figure 6b), then stack into the QSF 6 (Figure 6c).[76]
Our group demonstrated a QSF 7 constructed by a methylsubstituted cucurbit[6]uril derived from 3α-methyl-glycoluril (HMeQ[6]) in aqueous HCl solution. It was formed through the OSIQ and hydrogen bonding between latticed water molecules and HMeQ[6] molecules (omitted for clarity in Figure 7a), and the self-induced OSIQ between two adjacent HMeQ[6] molecules in the QSF is shown in Figure 7b. QSF 7 was full of rectangular channels, the inner walls of which were decorated of portal carbonyl oxygen atoms. Polarity plays a dominant role in their chemical behavior, and the channels could have a certain selectivity for different volatile substances via non-covalent surface adsorption. Figure $\mathtt{7c}$ shows sorption profiles of several volatile materials on the activated QSF 7 by vacuuming to a constant weight at $60-70^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . It exhibited more obvious selectivity for polar volatile materials, especially volatile alcohols. Moreover, recyclability tests revealed that this QSF was quite stable, with a larger sorption capacity for methanol. Accordingly, such QSFs may be useful in molecular sieves, sensors, absorption and separation.[77]
Kim and co-workers first found that Q[7] can form a hydrogel without modification of its periphery. They claimed that the gelation of Q[7] was thermoreversible, sensitive to pH, and inhibited by the presence of alkali metal ions. The driving force for gelation appeared to be the propensity of Q[7] to aggregate through strong interactions between Q[7] portals and hydronium ions. Hydrogen bonding occurred between the protonated water molecules and the portal carbonyl oxygen atoms, as well as between Q[7] molecules themselves. This is the self-induced OSIQ shown in Figure 3. The crystal structure of the QSF 8 is a good illustration of this effect. Figure 8a shows the OSIQ among the adjacent Q[7] molecules. Each Q[7] molecule in the framework interacts with six adjacent Q[7] molecules through the self-induced OSIQ, resulting in the formation of QSF 8 (Figure 8b). It was more important that the Q[7]-based hydrogel showed a guest-induced stimuli-responsive sol-gel transition. By choosing the appropriate guest molecules, one may be able to design various stimuli-responsive gel systems based on the intriguing host-guest chemistry of Q[7].[78]
When Lu and co-workers investigated the drug delivery function of Q[7], they obtained a drug free Q[7]-based QSF 9 (Figure 9a), it looks like bamboo fence (Figure 9b). Close inspection reveals that each Q[7] molecule in QSF 9 interacts with six adjacent Q[7] molecules through the self-induced OSIQ (Figure 9c), This framework was disassembled into different 2D Q[7]-based supramolecular frameworks from different directions (Figures 9d-f).[79]
Bardelang and co-workers investigated the solid state assemblies of Q[n]s, and found an odd-even effect in the crystallinity of thermally treated Q[n] samples (Q[5] and Q[7] were amorphous, while Q[6] and Q[8] were crystalline. This led to a more favorable solvation for the Q[n]s with odd symmetry. Those with even symmetry preferred to self-associate in a manner ultimately leading to crystallization. These driving forces were known as the OSIQ.[67] Indeed, Q[8] was able to form various solid supramolecular frameworks in different acidic media. We recently obtained two Q[8]-based QSFs 10 and 11 from an aqueous HCl solution (6M) and $H N O_{3}$ solution (3.5M), respectively. Figures 10a,b show each Q[8] molecule (in yellow) interacts with four (in the QSF 10) and eight (in the QSF11) Q[8] molecules through the self-induced OSIQ. By virtue of the self-induced OSIQ among Q[8] molecules and hydrogen bonding between Q[8] molecules and latticed water molecules, Q[8] self-assembled to form the novel supramolecular frameworks as shown in Figures 10c,d. Close inspection revealed that both 3D frameworks were disassembled into different 2D frameworks. Figures 10e,g show two different Q[8]-based layered structures which can be stacked and interleaved to form QSF 10; Figure 10f shows a layered 2D honeycomb-like framework in QSF 11, and numerous six-Q[8]- based hexagons existing in the 2D framework. Each large sixQ[8]-based hexagon was surrounded by six small three-Q[8]- based triangles, and is filled with water molecules and nitrate anions (omitted for clarity). Each hexagon in the QSF 11 was capped by triangles from the adjacent 2D frameworks to form a cage. In the next chapter, we will discuss the chemical guests that can be captured in such cages (Figure 10h).[80]
In 2009, Isaacs reported a Q[10]-based two-dimensional supramolecular framework in one of his featured articles (Figure 11b).[81] However, the synthetic conditions were not elaborated in this paper. Recent studies have indicated that Q[10] readily forms solid crystals with framework structural features via the selfinduced OSIQ between adjacent Q[10] molecules in different acidic media. For example, the Q[10]-based QSF 12, exactly the same as that prepared by Isaacs was formed in 6M HCl (Figure 11b). In $3M H N O_{3}$ , another two-dimensional Q[10]-based QSF 13 was formed (Figure 11e). The main driving force responsible for the formation of the QSFs 12 and 13 was self-induced OSIQ of adjacent Q[10] molecules, i.e., the dipole interactions between the electrostatic potential negative portal carbonyl oxygen atoms of the Q[10] molecules and the electrostatic potential positive outer surface of adjacent Q[10] molecules (Figure 11a,d). Close inspection revealed that there were numerous triangular branches in the QSF 12 and square branches in the QSF 13, that formed regions with a high density of portal carbonyl oxygen atoms (Figure 11c, f).[82] Hydrogen bonding between the solvent water molecules, HCl molecules filling in the branches and the portal carbonyl oxygen atoms of the Q[10] molecules in the branches also played an auxiliary role (the water molecules and HCl molecules are omitted for clarity). Generally, a smaller $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{n}]$ with a higher density of carbonyl oxygen atoms around its two open portals, such as Q[5] and Q[6], was more likely to coordinate to metal ions.[83,84] However, experimental results investigating the introduction of metal ions into the Q[10]-HCl and $\mathsf{Q}[10]\ –\mathsf{H N O}_{3}$ systems showed that the crystals obtained from the HCl medium had a 2D supramolecular framework similar to that of QSF 12, and metal ions were selectively embedded in the trianglular regions, whereas the crystals obtained from $H N O_{3}$ still showed the structural characteristics of QSF 13, and the square region was still occupied by solvent water molecules without any metal ions. By exploiting this difference, we could conveniently construct a cyclic system for the selective enrichment or separation of different metal ions (Figure 11g).[82]
Moreover, the two layered Q[10]-based QSFs 12 and 13 (Figure 12a, b) were used to adsorb different fluorescent dyes (FDs) to prepare fluorescent FD@QSF by adding activated solid QSFs to a fluorescent dye (FD) acetonitrile solution with stirring for 1 h and filtering. Thereby fabricating sensors with different fluorescence characteristics was able to display distinct responses to different compounds. For example, QSF 12 adsorbed 8-hydroxyquinoline (FD1) yielded a solid with a strong bright blue fluorescence. The solid responded almost selectively to pyridine by undergoing fluorescence quenching (Figure 12c, e), whereas QSF 13 adsorbed levofloxacin (FD2) gave solid $\mathtt{F D2}\ @13$ with a weak green fluorescence, which was enhanced in a highly selective response to ethanol (Figure 12d, f).[85]
## 2.2. QSFs assembled via the anion-induced OSIQ
The electrostatic potential positive outer surface of a Q[n] can interact with the carbonyl oxygen atoms of an adjacent $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{n}]$ but it was more likely to interact with electronegative species. It could, especially interact with anions through ion-dipole interactions, including interactions between the anions and methine units, bridged methylene units, the portal carbonyl carbon atoms of Q[n] molecule (Figure 13). In general, the QSFs prepared through the anion-induced OSIQ have high yields of $60-80\%$ . Under suitable conditions, $\mathsf Q[\boldsymbol n]$ molecules and anions easily form QSFs through OSIQs. For example, when transition metal ions were introduced into Q[n]-HCl systems, they can readily form polychlorinated transition metal anions, such as $[\mathsf{M}_{\mathsf{d}\cdot\mathsf{b}\mathsf{l o c k}}\mathsf{C l}_{4}]^{2-}$ anions, which play an important role in the construction of QSFs through the OSIQs. By introducing salts of $\mathsf{C d}^{2+}$ , $Z n^{2+}$ , or ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ into Q[8]-HCl $(5.8\mathrm{mol/L})$ systems, we obtained $\mathsf Q[8]$ -based $\mathsf{Q S F s}$ , that could be used as solid-phase microextraction fiber coating adsorbent materials for the analysis of trace polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons in actual water samples.[68,86-88]
Polyacids and heteropolyacids or polyoxometalates (abbreviated as HPAs) are another important class of compounds. Generally, HPAs are anionic in aqueous solution. They are, however, usually too large to be included within Q[n]s to form host-guest inclusion complexes. Therefore, they are ideal structure-directing agents in the construction of Q[n]-based porous supramolecular frameworks. For example, Kögerler and co-workers first reported two novel supramolecular frameworks constructed from the $[\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\ @\mathsf{V}^{\mathsf{I V}}\mathsf{\Pi}_{18}\mathsf{O}_{42}]^{12-}$ anion and Q[6] and Q[8], respectively in 2009. we have discussed them in more detail elsewhere.[89] Considering the inherent diversity of HPAs, as well as their various features, such as unique acidity, "quasi−liquid phase" behavior, redox properties, photocatalytic properties and other functional characteristics, research on Q[n]-HPAs-based supramolecular frameworks can be expected to be fruitful. This should not only enrich the research on OSIQs, but also impact Q[n]-chemistry and HPA-chemistry with new vitality. For example, Cao and co-workers demonstrated a series of supramolecular compounds constructed from decamethylcucurbit[5]uril $(\mathsf{M e}_{10}\mathsf{Q}[5])$ and different HPAs.[90] Among them was a $M e_{10}\mathsf{Q}[5].$ - $[\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{10}\mathsf{O}_{28}]^{4-}$ -based supramolecular framework (14) prepared by in situ hydrothermal reaction of $\mathsf{V O}_{3}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{M e}_{10}\mathsf{Q}[5]$ under pH range of 3.0-6.0 (Figures 14a,b). Close inspection revealed that each $\mathsf{M e}_{10}\mathsf{Q}[5]$ molecule (in yellow) interacts with four adjacent $\mathsf{M e}_{10}\mathsf{Q}[5]$ molecules and four $[\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{V}_{10}\mathsf{O}_{28}]^{4-}$ anions through both self-induced and anion-induced OSIQs(Figures 14c).[91] The further investigation of the functional properties revealed that the QSF 14 exhibited reversible photochromic properties as well as excellent photocatalytic activity toward the degradation of methyl orange and rhodamine B under visible light irradiation.[92]
Our group also demonstrated a series of Q[n]-HPAs-based compounds, some of them had the characteristic framework structure via the anion-induced OSIQ between $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{\eta}]$ and HPA anions. For example, a $(\mathsf{H O})_{10}\mathsf{Q}[5]$ -based supramolecular framework (15) was prepared using $[\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}]^{3^{-}}$ as a structure directing agent (Figure 15a). The detailed interactions between $(\mathsf{H O})_{10}\mathsf{Q}[5]$ and $[\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}]^{3^{-}}$ anions resulted from hydrogen bonding of oxygen atoms of $[\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}]^{3-}$ anions with hydroxyl units in $(\mathsf{H O})_{10}\mathsf{Q}[5]$ . In addition, further interactions were from the OSIQ between oxygen atoms of $[\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}]^{3-}$ anions and portal carbonyl carbon atoms, bridged methylene units on the outer surface of $(\mathsf{H O})_{10}\mathsf{Q}[5]$ molecules (Figure 15b).[93]
Polychlorinated transition metal anions are even more commonly used as structure directing agents to construct QSFs. For example, a $\mathsf{Q}[5]/[\mathsf{P t C}|_{6}]^{2-}$ -based porous supramolecular framework (16) was obtained from aqueous HCl solution (6.0 M) in the presence of $[P t C l_{6}]^{2-}$ ∙anions used as the structure directing agent (Figure 16a). The driving forces for the formation of QSF 16 were considered to be the self- and anion-induced OSIQs of a Q[5] (in yellow) with four adjacent Q[5] molecules and six $[P t C l_{6}]^{2-}$ anions (Figure 16b). A activated QSF 16 displayed a distinct absorption proclivity for tetrachloromethane, whereas the amorphous Q[5] powders based porous compound displayed absorption proclivity for methanol (Figure 16c). Lifetime tests on this porous material using methanol showed it to be stable.[94]
Isaacs and co-workers reported a Q[6] dimer synthesized by condensation of a 2:1 mixture of glycoluril hexamer with biphenyl${}_{3,3^{\prime},4,4^{\prime}}$ -tetracarbaldehyde in 9 M ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}{\sf O}_{4}$ at room temperature for 48 hours. The pure Q[6] dimer was isolated in with $34\%$ yield after Dowex ion-exchange chromatography and recrystallization from aqueous ${\mathsf{C F}}_{3}{\mathsf{C O}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}$ . Structure determination by single-crystal Xray diffraction revealed that Q[6] dimer-based compound had a framework structural characteristic (17) (Figures 17a,b). Close inspection revealed that both the self-induced OSIQ and trifluoroacetic anion-induced OSIQ existed among the adjacent Q[6] dimers (Figure 17c).[95]
$[P t C l_{6}]^{2-}$ anion resulted in the formation of Q[5]-based QSF 16 as shown in Figure 16a and also a Q[6]/[PtCl6]2−-based porous supramolecular framework (18) (Figure 18a). Each Q[6] interacted with four adjacent Q[6] molecules through the selfinduced OSIQ and six $[P t C l_{6}]^{2-}$ anions through the anion-induced OSIQ (Figure 18b). Considering the protruding carbonyl oxygens, which decorated the inner walls of the channels in QSF 18, the strong polarity of the channels exhibit a certain degree of selectivity for different volatile materials, especially polar volatile materials (Figure 18c left). In contrast, the inner walls of the channels in the Kim’s honeycomb-like Q[6]-based porous supramolecular framework were decorated by the outer surface of Q[6] molecules (Figure 4a) and may thus exhibit possible selectivity for other compounds (Figure 18c right).[96]
Our group synthesized a symmetric octamethyl-substituted cucurbit[6]uril (OMeQ[6]). It was synthesized in a controlled manner by using a dimer of dimethyl-substituted glycoluril. Using an approach involving anion-induced construction of QSFs, we were fortunate to obtain single crystals of OMeQ[6] in the presence of $[\mathsf{C d}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{7}]^{3-}$ anions used as a structure-directing agent.[68,83,84] In the OMeQ[6]-based supramolecular framework (19), each OMeQ[6] molecule interacted with four adjacent OMeQ[6] molecules through self-induced OSIQ and interacted with six adjacent $[C\mathsf{d}_{2}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{l}_{7}]^{3-}$ anions through anion-induced OSIQ (Figure $19a)$ . In addition, there was hydrogen bonding between OMeQ[6] molecules and latticed water molecules in the framework (omitted in Figure 19 for clarity). Although each Q[5] Q[6] or OMeQ[6] molecule was surrounded by six $[\mathsf{P t C l}_{6}]^{2-[94,96]}$ or $[\mathsf{C d}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{7}]^{3-}$ anions through the anion-induced OSIQ and the substituted methyl groups obviously affected the locations of the $[\mathsf{C d}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{7}]^{3-}$ anions such that the arrangement of OMeQ[6] molecules was different from that of Q[5 or $6]/[\mathsf{P t C l}_{6}]^{2-}$ -based supramolecular frameworks, the channel in QSF 19 is stacked by OMeQ[6] molecules and every $[\mathsf{C d}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{7}]^{3-}$ anion chain is surrounded by four OMeQ[6]-based channels (Figure 19b).[97]
Liu and co-workers investigated the coordination of TMeQ[6] with $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ in the presence of $[\mathsf{C u C l}_{4}]^{2-}$ as structure directing agent in different concentration of aqueous HCl solutions. They obtained a TMeQ[6]-based supramolecular framework (20) which was a similar structure to QSF 19. Each channel was stacked by TMeQ[6] molecules and each $\mathsf{[C u C l_{4}]^{2-}}$ -based anion chain is surrounded by four TMeQ[6]-based channels (Figure 20a). Each TMeQ[6] molecule (in yellow) interacted with four adjacent TMeQ[6] molecules through the self-induced OSIQ, and eight $\mathsf{[C u C l_{4}]^{2-}}$ anions through the anion-induced OSIQ (Figure 20b).[76]
The $\mathsf{[C d C l_{4}]^{2-}}$ and other anions used as structure directing agents have proven to be useful in the construction of cucurbit[n]uril-metal coordination architectures and materials.[68,83,84] In order to better understand the role and influence of these anions in $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{\eta}]$ systems, we demonstrated herein another Q[6]-based QSF 21 in the presence of $\mathsf{[C d C l_{4}]^{2-}}$ anions. X-ray diffraction analysis revealed that each Q[6] molecule interacted with four adjacent Q[6] molecules through self-induced OSIQ, and interacted with five $\mathsf{[C d C l_{4}]^{2\mathrm{-}}}$ anions through theanion-induced OSIQ (Figure 21a). Q[6] molecules and $\mathsf{[C d C l_{4}]^{2-}}$ anions were arranged into QSF 21 with numerous channels, the pore of which was composed of four Q[n]s. $\scriptstyle[\mathsf{C d C l}_{4}]^{2}$ anions, which played the role of a hinge to link Q[6] molecules in the channels (Figure 21b).[98]
Unlike the cases where the methyl-substituted Q[6] are stacked into channels in the frameworks (Figures 19 and 20), when Xiao and co-workers investigated interaction of antituberculosis drug isoniazid (INH) with TMeQ[6] in the presence of $[{\mathsf{C d C l}}_{4}]^{2-}$ anion, they obtained a TMeQ[6]/[CdCl4]2−-based supramolecular framework (22) (Figure 22a). In this framework, each TMeQ[6] molecule not only interacted with two adjacent TMeQ[6] molecules and eight $[{\mathsf{C d C l}}_{4}]^{2-}$ anions through the selfand anion-induced OSIQs, but also formed an exclusion complex with four INH molecules through the hydrogen bonding of the INH with the portal carbonyl oxygen atoms (Figures 22b-e). This form was a new SQ[6]-based supramolecular framework, different from 19 and 20.[99]
For the unsubstituted Q[5 or 6]s, the polychlorinated transition metal anion-induced supramolecular frameworks seemed to favorably present such channels, the pore of which was composed of four Q[n]s; whereas for the alkyl-substituted Q[6]s (SQ[6]s), the polychlorinated transition metal anion-induced supramolecular frameworks seemed to favorite the present SQ[6]-stacked channels, whose pore was controlled by the portal of the SQ[n]s. The QSF 23 was constructed by the inverted Q[6] (iQ[6]) in the presence of tetrachlorozincate anion $([Z\mathsf{n C l4}]^{2-})$ anion as the structure directing agent. Each iQ[6] molecule also interacted with four adjacent iQ[6] molecules through the selfinduced OSIQ. It interacted with five $[Z\mathsf{n C l}_{4}]^{2-}$ anions through the anion-induced OSIQ (Figure 23a). Combining these OSIQs, iQ[6] molecules and $[Z\mathsf{n C l}_{4}]^{2^{-}}$ anions could arrange into 2D frameworks (Figure 23b), which then further stacked into the QSF 23 (Figure 23c).[100]
The following works revealed that iQ[6] could form various supramolecular frameworks in the presence of a series of $^{1,\omega}$ alkyldiamines and $[Z\mathsf{n C l}_{4}]^{2-}$ anion as structure directing agents, respectively. For example, the presence of ethylenediamine and $[Z\mathsf{n C l}_{4}]^{2-}$ anion resulted in the formation of an $i{\sf Q}[6]/[{\sf Z}\sf n{\sf C}\sf l_{4}]^{2-\tau_{-}}$ based supramolecular framework (24) in which each iQ[6] molecule (in yellow) interacted with four iQ[6] molecules and five $[Z\mathsf{n C l}_{4}]^{2-}$ anions through the self- and anion-induced OSIQs, moreover, the iQ[6] molecule formed an exclusion complex with an ethylenediamine molecule via hydrogen bonding (Figure 24a). These OSIQs promoted the formation of iQ[6]/[ZnCl4]2−-based 2D supramolecular framework, in which there were two types of holes constructed by four iQ[6] molecules: 1) the inner walls of the larger holes were decorated by portal carbonyl oxygen atoms of four iQ[6] molecules and an ethylenediamine molecule is located in; 2) the inner walls of the smaller holes were decorated by the outer surface of four iQ[6] molecules and two $[Z\mathsf{n C l}_{4}]^{2-}$ anions (Figure 24b). The stacking of $i{\sf Q}[6]/[Z{\sf n C l}_{4}]^{2-}$ -based 2D supramolecular frameworks further created QSF 24 (Figure 24c). The presence of $[Z n C l_{4}]^{2}$ − anion and 1,4-butanediamine and 1,6- hexylenediamine, respectively resulted in the formation of another set of two different QSFs (25 and 26) (Figures 24d,e). Each iQ[6] molecule interacted with four and six adjacent iQ[6] molecules, and five and six $[Z\mathsf{n C l}_{4}]^{2-}$ anions, respectively (Figures 24f,g). Thus, it seemed to be favored for the iQ[6] to form frameworks in which the pore of channels was composed of four iQ[6] molecules.[101]
Recently, Fedin and co-workers demonstrated a series of novel QSFs and mono-/polynuclear bismuth(III)-, and mercury(II)- ions, which were structurally characterized by single-crystal X-ray diffraction. They are $({\mathsf{H}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}})_{4}[{\mathsf{B i}}_{2}{\mathsf{C l}}_{10}]\cdot$ Q[6]∙14H2O (27), $(H_{3}O)_{4}[\mathsf{B i C l}_{6}]\cdot\mathsf{C l}\cdot\mathsf{Q}[6]\cdot8\mathsf{H}_{2}O$ (28), $(H_{3}O)_{6}(C_{6}H_{8}N)[B i$ Br6] Br∙2Q[6] $2H_{2}O$ (29) and $(H_{3}O)_{3}[H g_{2}C l_{7}]\cdot O[6]\cdot6H2O$ (30). The crystal structures of 27-30 showed that packs of mono/polynuclear ions and Q[6] lead to the formation variable diameter channels ranging from 4 to $8\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ . The channels were occupied by disordered hydronium cations and water molecules. For QSF 27, each Q[6] molecule interacted with four $[\mathsf{B i}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{10}]^{4-}$ anions through the anion-induced OSIQ (Figure 25a). By virtue of the anioninduced OSIQ and hydrogen bonding of latticed water molecules with Q[6] molecules, Q[6] molecules and $[\mathsf{B i}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{10}]^{4-}$ anions were arranged into a honeycomb-like 3D supramolecular framework (Figure 25b). QSFs 28 and 29 had similar interaction modes among the Q[6] molecules and mono bismuth(III) anions and similar structural characteristics. Each Q[6] molecule interacted with six adjacent Q[6] molecules and six [BiCl or $\mathsf{B r}_{6}\mathsf{J}^{3-}$ anions through the self- and anion-induced OSIQs (Figures 25c,e). Thus, Q[6] molecules and [BiCl or $\mathsf{B r}_{6}\mathsf{J}^{3-}$ anions could arrange into 3D QSFs (Figures 25d,f). Each Q[6] molecule in QSF 30 interacted with four adjacent Q[6] molecules and four $[\mathsf{H g}_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{14}]^{6-}$ anions through the self- and anion-induced OSIQs (Figure 25g). In addition, hydrogen bonding of latticed water molecules with Q[6] molecules resulted in the formation of QSF 30 (Figure 25h). This work further extended the research on the construction of QSFs assembled through the anion-induced OSIQs.[102]
We recently reported two hybrid QSFs 31 and 32 of perhydroxylated cucurbit[6]uril $\{(mathsf{H O})_{12}\mathsf{Q}[6]\}$ and Keggin-type heteropolyacids (HPAs) (such as $\mathsf{H}_{3}[\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}]$ and $\mathsf{H}_{3}[\mathsf{P W}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}])$ by mixing them in 1M aqueous HCl solution. X-ray single crystal analysis revealed that they are isomorphs, and herein we took the $(\mathsf{H O})_{12}\mathsf{Q}[6]/[\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}]^{3-}$ case (31) as representative. Each $(H O)_{12}\mathsf{Q}[6]$ molecule interacted with six $[\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}]^{3-}$ anions. In turn, each $[\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}]^{3-}$ anion interacted with six $(H O)_{12}\mathsf{Q}[6]$ molecules through hydrogen bonding. Direct interactions between $(H O)_{12}\mathsf{Q}[6]$ molecules or $[\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}]^{3-}$ anions were not observed because the two building blocks were large and consequently obstruct each other (Figures 26a,b). $(H O)_{12}\mathsf{Q}[6]$ molecules and $[\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}]^{3-}$ anions were arranged into 3D QSF through hydrogen bonding (Figures 26c). Property tests revealed that the $(H O)_{12}Q I$ [6]-POMs system may offer a feasible route for the uptake and release of POMs, exhibited esterification and aqueous dyes degradation catalytic activities, and adsorbed volatile organic compounds, yielding adsorbing materials with interesting photochemical properties.[103]
Zhang and co-workers reported two Q[7]-based QSFs 33 and 34 which exploited the tendency of chaotropic anions $(\mathsf{B}_{12}\mathsf{H}_{12}{}^{2-})$ to associate with the electrostatic potential positive outer surface of Q[7]. In QSF 33, each Q[7] molecule interacted with six adjacent Q[7] molecules, two $\mathsf{B}_{12}\mathsf{H}_{12}{}^{2-}$ anions interacted through the self and anion-induced OSIQs (Figure 27a), resulting in the formation of 33 (Figure 27b). In QSF 34, each Q[7] molecule interacted with four adjacent Q[7] molecules, eight $\mathsf{B}_{12}\mathsf{H}_{12}{}^{2-}$ anions through the self and anion-induced OSIQs (Figure 27c), and there were two different channels: the first was stacked by Q[7] molecules, the second one was comprised by six Q[7]-based channels, and a string of $\mathsf{B}_{12}\mathsf{H}_{12}{}^{2-}$ anions were included (Figure 27d). In these frameworks, dodecaborate anions formed exclusion complexes with Q[7] driven by the anion-induced OSIQ, leaving the cavity accessible to bind other organic guest molecules. The multi-responsive assemblies were conveniently assembled in aqueous solution at ambient temperature, establishing a new platform to prepare supramolecular frameworks under mild conditions.[104]
Using the $[\mathsf{M}_{\sf d-b l o c k}\mathsf{C l}_{4}]^{2}$ − anion-induced strategy, we synthesized a series of Q[8]-based porous supramolecular frameworks $(M_{d-b l o c k}=Z n$ , Ni, Cd). The driving forces of the structure-directing effect of $[\mathsf{M}_{\mathsf{d-b l o c k}}\mathsf{C l}_{4}]^{2-}$ arose from the anioninduced OSIQ and hydrogen bonding between water molecules and Q[8] molecules. Herein, we take the $\mathsf{[C d C l_{4}]^{2-}}$ case (35) as a representative compound. Each Q[8] molecule interacted with two adjacent Q[8] molecules and seven $[\mathsf{C d C l}_{4}]^{2-}$ anions through the self- and anion-induced OSIQs (Figure 28a) and Q[8] molecules and $[{\mathsf{C d C l}}_{4}]^{2-}$ anions constructed the QSF 35 with numerous parallel quadrilateral channels (Figure 28b). Notably, tests of potential applications showed that these porous frameworks could both capture organic molecules through the cavity of Q[8] moieties and also adsorb larger organic molecules with different selectivity. Accordingly, these Q[8]-based porous supramolecular frameworks were further investigated as extraction coatings on solid-phase microextraction (SPME) fibers. This was done to examine polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs) in aqueous samples. The results indicated that the fiber with Q[8]-based porous crystals exhibited excellent extraction efficiency and stability, and could thus be applied in the analysis of trace PAHs in real water samples (Figure 28c).[88] Furthermore, QSF 35 was used as stationary phase for capillary gas chromatography (GC). The separation performance showed that it exhibited good separation mainly for nonpolar to weakly polar analytes(Figure 28d).[105]
After we obtained the honeycomb-like Q[8]-based supramolecular framework (Figure 10), we also obtained a similar honeycomb-like Q[8]-based framework (36) from aqueous HCl and $H N O_{3}$ solutions that contain $[A u C l_{4}]^{-}$ anions. The main driving force for the synthesis was the self- and anion-induced OSIQs derived from the planer inorganic anions $[A u C l_{4}]^{-}$ and ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}{}^{-}$ to result in the formation of QSF 36. Figure 29a shows the detailed OSIQs between Q[8] molecules and $[A u C l_{4}]^{-}$ anions in QSF 36. Each Q[8] molecule interacted with eight adjacent Q[8] molecules and 10 $[A u C l_{4}]^{-}$ anions. Figure 29b shows the structure of QSF 36. The 3D framework was made of stacked 2D honeycomb-like frameworks, and six $[A u C l_{4}]^{-}$ anions are hosted in the six-Q[8]- based hexagon (Figure 29c). A series of co-precipitation experiments revealed that QSF 36 exhibits high selectivity for $[A u C l_{4}]^{-}$ anions. The 2D Q[8]-based honeycomb-like frameworks were suitable to accommodate planar anions like [AuCl4]. Thus, combination of the reduction process by using hydrazine hydrate, and a recycling process for the capture and recovery of gold is illustrated in Figure 29d.[80]
The $[\mathsf{M}_{\mathsf{d-b i o c k}}\mathsf{C l}_{4}]^{2-}$ anions inducing strategy was suitable for various $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{\eta}]$ systems to manifest as QSFs, including Q[5]-, Q[6] and its alkyl-substitued Q[6]-, Q[7]- and Q[8]-systems. Herein, we introduced a Q[10]-based supramolecular framework (37), which was obtained by dissolving Q[10] molecule in aqueous HCl solution (4M) in the presence of $[{\mathsf{C d C l}}_{4}]^{2-}$ anions. In this framework, each Q[10] molecule interacted with eight adjacent Q[10] molecules through the self-induced OSIQ and four $[\mathsf{C d C l_{4}}]^{2-}$ anions through the anion-induced OSIQ (Figure 30a). Each channel in QSF 37 was stacked by Q[10] molecules, and every four adjacent Q[10]-stacked channels created a small channel filling with a $[{\mathsf{C d C l}}_{4}]^{2-}$ anion string (Figure 30b).[106]
When a solution of Q[10] in 8M HCl was added to a solution of $\mathsf{C d C l}_{2}$ in 8M HCl under agitation, a microcrystalline precipitate immediately separated. Structural determination indicated that this compound (38) was constructed through the outer surface interactions of Q[10] molecules with $\mathsf{[C d_{4}C l_{16}]^{8-}}$ cluster anions, and constituted a typical Q[10]-based QSF (Figure 31a). Figure 31b shows its topological structure, which can be viewed as a stack of parallelepiped cages. Each cage was composed of six Q[10] molecules and twelve $\mathsf{[C d_{4}C l_{16}]^{8-}}$ cluster anions (Figure 31c,d). Close inspection revealed that each Q[10] molecule interacted with four $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{C d}_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{16}]^{8-}$ cluster anions (Figure 31e), and each $[\mathsf{C d}_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{16}]^{8-}$ cluster anion interacted with four Q[10] molecules (Figure 31f). This assembly could be attributed to the OSIQs, specifically, the ion-dipole interaction between the electrostatic potential positive outer surface of Q[10] and $[\mathsf{C d}_{4}\mathsf{C l}_{16}]^{8-}$ cluster anions. The Q[10]-based QSF could accommodate various FDs to prepare fluorescent $F D@\bigcirc\bigcirc\mathsf{S}F$ by adding an activated solid QSF to a FD acetonitrile solution with stirring for $1\ h$ and filtering. After adsorption of rhodamine B (FD3), pyrenemethanamine hydrochloride (FD4), and bathocuproine hydrochloride (FD5) respectively, solid materials with red, green, and blue fluorescence were obtained. There three primary color sources could be used in fabricating multi-emitting fluorescent materials (Figure 32).[107]
