When the structure directing agent $\mathsf{[C d C l_{4}]^{2-}}$ was replaced by $[Z n C l_{4}]^{2-}$ , a new Q[10]-based QSF 39 was obtained from a Q[10]- $H C l-Z n C l_{2}$ solution. Figure 33a-h shows the detailed interaction of four different types of Q[10] molecules interacting through the selfinduced OSIQ and with $[Z\mathsf{n C l}_{4}]^{2-}$ anions through anion-induced OSIQ. Figure 33i shows QSF 39 and its topological structure (Figure 33j). Considering the porous structure of QSF 39 and referring to the adsorption properties for various FDs of QSF 38, we loaded activated QSF 39 with various FDs, and fluorescent FD@QSFs can be easily obtained by above mentioned method. The FDs included various dyes and fused-ring compounds. The moieties yielded solid luminescent materials that exhibited varying degrees of fluorescence enhancement/quenching or emission color changes. A solid luminescent material loading with 1,8-diaminonaphthalene (FD6) exhibited a distinct response to formaldehyde and could be used as a solid sensor (Figure 33k).[108]
Instead of adding Ln $^{3+}$ ,[31,36] introducing 1,12- dodecanedipyridyl into a twisted cucurbit[14]uril (tQ[14])/CdCl2/HCl system yielded a $t\mathsf{Q}[14]/[\mathsf{C d}_{6}\mathsf{C l}_{19}]^{7-}$ -based QSF 40. Although the organic guest 1,12-dodecanedipyridyl was not found in the framework, it likely played some unclear role in the construction of QSF 40. Each tQ[14] molecule interacted with four adjacent tQ[14] molecules and two $[\mathsf{C d}_{6}\mathsf{C l}_{19}]^{7-}$ cluster anions through the self-induced OSIQ (Figure 34a); in turn, the $[\mathsf{C d}_{6}\mathsf{C l}_{19}]^{7-}$ cluster anion held six adjacent $t\mathsf{Q}[14]$ molecules through the anion-induced OSIQ (Figure 34b). With these OSIQ, tQ[14] molecules and $[\mathsf{C d}_{6}\mathsf{C l}_{19}]^{7-}$ cluster anions formed 2D supramolecular frameworks in which every eight tQ[14] molecules construct a square hole, and the holes included four anions alternatively (Figure 34c). The 2D frameworks was further alternatively stacked into a 3D supramolecular framework (Figure 34d).[109]
## 2.3. QSFs assembled via the aromatic-induced OSIQ
Aromatic compounds, especially those containing carboxyl groups, can interact with the electrostatic potential positive outer surface of Q[n]s through hydrogen bonding, πinteractions, and C-Hπ interactions (Figure 35). In general, the QSFs prepared through the aromatic-induced OSIQ have yields of $40\mathrm{-}60\%$ . A combination of this type of OSIQ with the previous mentioned two types of OSIQs as shown in Figure 3 and Figure 13 manifests in the formation of various QSFs.
The $p$ -hydroxybenzoic acid (Hyb) has proven to be a very good structure directing agent in our research on Q[n]-based coordination chemistry. For example, we first introduced Hyb into a Q[5]- $\cdot\mathsf{K}^{+}$ -system and obtained a 2D coordination framework, which exhibited highly selective methanol absorption.[110] Further experiments using X-ray diffraction analysis showed that a mixture of Q[5] and Hyb without metal salt in aqueous HCl solution, produced Q[5]/Hyb-based QSF 41 (Figure 36a). Figure 36b shows comprehensive interactions between Q[5] molecules and Hyb molecules. Each Q[5] molecule interacted with four adjacent Q[5] molecules through the self-induced OSIQ (Figure 36c), two Hyb molecules through the anion-induced OSIQ (Figure 36d), and four Hyb molecules through the aromatic-induced OSIQ (Figure 36e). Thus, a combination of these three types of OSIQs as well as the hydrogen bonding of latticed water molecules and portal carbonyl oxygen atoms (omitted for clarity) facilitated the formation of the QSF 41.[111]
Hydroquinone (Hyq) can serve as an aromatic structure directing agent used in place of the previously used $p\mathrm{-}$ hydroxybenzoic acid (Hyb). For example, introducing Hyq into Q[5]- $L n^{3+}$ systems can lead to the formation of one-dimensional coordination polymers. This is especially true for systems containing heavy lanthanide ions, such as $\mathsf{D}\mathsf{y}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{E r}^{3+}$ , $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ , and ${\mathsf{L}}{\mathsf{U}}^{3+}$ , with which homochiral one-dimensional helical coordination polymers can be obtained.[112] By introducing Hyq into the Q[7]- $\mathsf{R b^{+}}$ system, metal-organic frameworks with three-dimensional structure can be obtained.[113] When Hyq was introduced into an aqueous solution containing a hemimethyl−substituted cucurbit[5]uril (SPMeQ[5]), an SPMeQ[5]-based QSF (42) was generated (Figure 37a, b). The driving force was attributed to the dipole interaction between the hydroxyl group on Hyq and the bridging methylene unit on the outer surface of SPMeQ[5] and the C-H∙∙∙π interaction between the aromatic ring of Hyq and the substituted methyl group on the outer surface of SPMeQ[5] (Figure 37c, d). In terms of channel structure, two inner channel walls were surrounded by portal carbonyl oxygen atoms of SPMeQ[5]. The channel passage should show polarity and accordingly, a strong affinity for polar molecules. This was consistent with the experimental finding that activated QSF 42 had a high adsorption capacity for alcohols, such as methanol and ethanol (Figure 37e).[114]
The most typical example was reported by Sun and coworkers, who used $4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}$ -benzene-1,3,5-tribenzoic acid (H3BTB) as a structure directing agent to synthesize 2D honeycomb-like Q[6]- and Q[7]-based QSFs. Figures 38a, b show the Q[6]-based QSF 43, in which three Q[6] molecules interacting with a BTB3− anion constituted the basic building block (Figure 38c). The interactions within the building block were complicate, including those between the BTB3- anion and the Q[6]s, those between the Q[6]s themselves, and those between water molecules and the Q[6]s. More specifically, there were: i) π-interactions between the aromatic benzoic acid rings of the ${\mathsf{B T B}}^{3-}$ anion and the carbonyl groups of the pentacyclic ring of Q[6] (Figure 38c); ii) C-Hπ interactions between the aromatic benzoic acid rings of the BTB3- anion and bridging methylene or waist methine groups; and iii) ion-dipole interactions between the carboxylic anion in ${\mathsf{B T B}}^{3-}$ and the positive electrostatic potential outer surfaces of adjacent Q[6] molecules. Together, these interactions allowed the ${\mathsf{B T B}}^{3-}$ anion to maintain attractive interaction with three Q[6] molecules (Figure 38c). iv) Furthermore, there were also dipole interactions between the positive electrostatic potential outer surfaces of the Q[6] molecules and the portal carbonyl oxygen atoms of the adjacent Q[6] molecules in the building block (Figure 38d). All of the above interactions were the typical outer surface interaction of Q[n]s. v) Finally, the hydrogen−bond network formed between the water molecules, the ammonium ions produced by neutralizing the acidic system with aqueous ammonia in the building block also played a role (Figure 38e). Further study on the functional properties revealed that when the Q[6]-based QSF was introduced into an alkali metal salt mixture under neutral environmental conditions, it could selectively trap cesium ion $(\mathsf{C}\mathsf{s}^{+})$ and then release trapped ${{\mathsf{C}}}{{\mathsf{s}}}^{+}$ in an acidic environment. ${{\mathsf{C}}}{{\mathsf{s}}}^{+}$ has the largest ionic radius among common alkali metals. In the process of capture and release, the QSF 43 maintained its original structure (Figure 38f). This recycling process had the potential to be used for cesium ion capture, especially in the context of radioactive decay.[115]
Calix[n]arenes (C[n]As) are characterized by a hydrophobic cavity and n phenyl rings which are bridged by n methylene groups. Thus, C[n]As could be ideal building blocks with aromatic structure directing agents used for the construction of Q[n]/C[n]Abased supramolecular frameworks. Generally, strong OSIQs between Q[n]s and C[n]As lead to the formation of a Q[n]/C[n]Abased precipitate. Long and co-workers demonstrated the assembly of two supramolecular frameworks using the supramolecular building blocks of 4-sulfocalix[n]arenes (SC[n]As, $\scriptstyle\mathsf{n}=4,6$ ) and Q[6]. The frameworks were synthesized via the selfand aromatic-induced OSIQs. They collected white precipitate form SC[4]A- or SC[6]A-Q[6] systems in a lower concentration HCl solution, then dissolved the SC[4]A/Q[6] precipitate in higher concentration HCl solution. Single crystals were formed after several days. On the other hand, dissolving the SC[6]A/Q[6] precipitate with ammonia allowed single crystals to form, again after several days. Single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis revealed the two frameworks, $\mathrm{2[6]/SC[4]A{\cdot}0.5H C|{\cdot}21.75H_{2}O}$ (44) and $(0.5\mathsf{Q}[6])_{2}/\mathsf{S C}[6]\mathsf{A}\cdot6\mathsf{N H}_{4}\cdot35.75\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ (45). These frameworks clearly showed the directing role that the different OSIQs had in the synthesis of these novel frameworks. Figures 39a-d shows the detailed OSIQ between Q[6] and SC[4]A, including a Q[6] molecule (in yellow) with adjacent two Q[6] molecules through the self-induced OSIQ, four SC[4]A molecules through the anioninduced OSIQ, and four SC[4]A molecules through the aromatic compound-induced OSIQ. Combination all of these three types of OSIQs was possible, and there was also hydrogen bonding between the latticed water mole and both building blocks. The combination of these interactions resulted in the formation of the novel Q[6]/SC[4]A-based supramolecular framework (Figure 39e). Figure 39f shows the detailed OSIQ between Q[6] and SC[6]A, including a Q[6] molecule (in yellow) with adjacent four SC[6]A molecules through the anion-induced OSIQ, and the aromatic compound-induced OSIQ. Again, the combination all of these two types of OSIQs, as well as hydrogen bonding between the latticed water mole and both building blocks, resulting in the formation of the novel Q[6]/SC[6]A-based supramolecular framework (Figure 39g).[116] Recently, our group demonstrated a Q[7]/SC[4]A-based QSF (46) upon mixing of aqueous solutions of water soluble Q[7] and SC[4]A, white microcrystals instantly separated in nearquantitative yield. The driving force for this assembly was suggested to be the OSIQs. Experimental results confirmed that the interaction ratio of Q[7] and SC[4]A was close to 3:1. Moreover, QSF 46 accommodated some volatile compounds and luminescent dyes. Thus, this work offered a simple and highly efficient means of preparing adsorbent or solid fluorescent materials.[117]
Hao and co-workers demonstrated a highly ordered 3D framework from 1,5-naphthalenedi-sulfonate (1,5-NDS) and Q[6] (47). Single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis revealed that the 1,5-NDS anions act as linkers that connect the Q[6] blocks together through comprehensive interactions (Figure 40a). Each Q[6] molecule interacted with four adjacent Q[6] molecules through the self-induced OSIQ (Figure 40b), the Q[6] molecule interacted with four adjacent 1,5-NDS molecules through the anion-induced OSIQ (Figure 40c), and the Q[6] molecule interacted with two adjacent 1,5-NDS molecules through the aromatic-induced OSIQ (Figure 40d). A combination of these OSIQs as well as hydrogen bonding between latticed water molecules and portal carbonyl oxygen atoms of Q[6] molecules resulted in the formation of the QSF 47 with numerous channels. Each channel was constructed by four Q[6]-based columns. Close inspection revealed that there are two different channels: one was filled with one 1,5-NDS string, the other was filled with two 1,5-NDS strings (Figure 40e). Relative to that observed for 1,5-NDS, the assembly exhibited a blue shift in the fluorescence wavelength from $\lambda~=~406$ to $340\ \mathsf{n m}$ . This suggested that Q[n]/Dye-based supramolecular frameworks could be used as solid fluorescence materials.[118]
Recently, Hao and co-workers demonstrated another Q[7]/1,5-NDS-based 3D QSF 48 by linkage of 1,5-NDS anion fluorophore with Q[7]. Figure 41a shows the overall interaction of a Q[7] molecule (in yellow) with adjacent Q[7] molecules and 1,5- NDS molecules. The Q[7] molecule interacted with seven adjacent Q[7] molecules through self-induced OSIQ (Figure 41b), and three adjacent 1,5-NDS molecules through the anion-induced (Figure 41c), and two adjacent 1,5-NDS molecules through the aromatic-induced OSIQ (Figure 41d). These OSIQs and additional hydrogen bonding between latticed water molecules with Q[7] results in the formation of QSF 48 (Figure 41e). Further experiments showed that the maximum solid-state fluorescence wavelength of QSF 48 exhibited a blue-shift from 406 to $340~\mathsf{n m}$ relative to that of 1,5-NDS. This shift was ascribed to an increased excitation energy from 0.10 to 0.13 eV according to theoretical calculations.[119]
Thuéry demonstrated a type of QSFs that was constructed from Q[n]s and complexes through comprehensive OSIQs. In particular, the complexes contained ligands with aromatic rings or carboxyl moieties. The complexes induced the $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{\eta}]$ to form supramolecular frameworks. In turn, Q[n]s also induced the complexes to form novel supramolecular frameworks and coordination polymers. For example, Thuéry obtained two QSFs from the hydrothermal reaction of uranyl nitrate with HOOC$({\mathsf{C H}}_{2})_{8^{-}}{\mathsf{C O O H}}$ $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{C}10)$ and HOOC- $(C H_{2})_{5}$ -COOH $(H_{2}C7)$ in the presence of Q[6], and dimethylformamide (DMF), which generated dimethylammonium cations in situ, respectively. The crystal structure of a $\mathsf{Q}[6]/\mathsf{U O}_{2}/\mathsf{C}10$ -based QSF (49) is shown in Figure 42a. Each Q[6] molecule was surrounded by seven $[(\mathsf{U O}_{2})(\mathsf{C O}_{2})_{3}]^{-}$ anion nodes from seven $\mathsf{U O}_{2}/\mathsf{C}10$ coordination polymers. The interactions between Q[6] molecules and the $[(\mathsf{U O}_{2})(\mathsf{C O}_{2})_{3}]^{-}$ anion nodes in $\mathsf{U O}_{2}/\mathsf{C}10$ coordination polymers belonged to the anion-induced OSIQ (Figure 42b). Interestingly, the presence of Q[6] resulted in the formation of a supramolecular framework of $\mathsf{U O}_{2}/\mathsf{C}10$ . It was a typical example of the Q[n]- induced formation of a complex-based supramolecular framework (Figure 42c). Figures 43a,b show the Q[ $6]/\mathsf{U O}_{2}/\mathsf{C}7$ -based QSF 50 which exhibited some differences from QSF 49, in which Q[6] molecules were isolated from each other by the $\mathsf{U O}_{2}/\mathsf{C}10$ -based supramolecular framework. In QSF 50, each Q[6] molecule interacted with four adjacent Q[6] molecules through the selfinduced OSIQ, and four adjacent $\mathsf{U O}_{2}/\mathsf{C}7$ -based anion nodes through the anion-induced OSIQ (Figure 43c). These OSIQs along with hydrogen bonding resulted in the formation of a 2D Q[6]-based supramolecular framework (Figure 43d) and 2D $\cup{\mathsf{O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C}}7$ -based supramolecular framework (Figure 43e). Figures 43f,g show the Q[6]-based (omitted $\cup{\mathsf{O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C}}7$ part) and $\cup{\mathsf{O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C}}7-$ based (omitted Q[6]) supramolecular frameworks, respectively.[120]
By reacting terbium nitrate with 1,3-adamantanedicarboxylic acid (LH2) and Q[6] under solvothermal conditions with N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF) as the organic solvent, yield compound 51. Single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis revealed that this compound is a 3D supramolecular framework (Figure 44a) similar to that as shown in Figure 43. The framework formed as alternately stacked carboxylate-bridged anionic 2D planar $T b(L)$ assemblies and layers of Q[6] molecules (Figure 44b). Each Q[6] molecule interacted with six adjacent Q[6] molecules through the self-induced OSIQ, and four adjacent $T{\mathsf{b}}/({\mathsf{L}})({\mathsf{H C O O}})_{2}$ -based anion nodes through the anion-induced OSIQ (Figure 44c). These OSIQs along with hydrogen bonding resulted in the formation of the 2D Q[6]-based supramolecular framework (Figure 44d) and the 2D ${\mathsf{T b}}/({\mathsf{L}})({\mathsf{H C O O}})_{2}$ -based supramolecular framework (Figure
44e). Figures 44f,g show QSF 51 omitted Tb(L) and omitted Q[6], respectively.[121]
Interestingly, when Thuéry worked on the Q[6]- $\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{U O}_{2}$ -Cnbased supramolecular assemblies, he found Q[8]- ${\mathsf{.U O}}_{2}$ -Cn-based QSF 52 by accident. This occurred because Q[8] came from bulk Q[6]. The crystal structure of this framework displayed an alternate stacking of planar uranyl organic two-dimensional layers with sheets of Q[8] molecules (Figure 45a). Figure 45b presents the comprehensive interactions of a Q[8] molecule with four adjacent Q[8] molecules through the self-induced OSIQ (Figure 45c) and $(\mathsf{U O}_{2})_{4}\mathsf{O}_{2}(\mathsf{C}_{6})_{3}]_{3}$ anion nodes in the $(\mathsf{U O}_{2})_{4}\mathsf{O}_{2}(\mathsf{C}_{6})_{3}]_{3}.$ - based coordination polymers through the anion-induced OSIQ (Figure 45d). Figures 45e and f show the Q[8]-based 2D supramolecular framework and the planar uranyl organic twodimensional coordination framework dissembled from QSF 52 as shown in Figure 45a, respectively.[120] Thuéry’s works provided new examples of the potential of Q[n]s as uranyl complexants or as structure-directing agents. The sheets or columns readily formed by Q[n]s, either free or complexed, appeared particularly well suited to associate with two-dimensional or ribbon-like topologies most common among uranylorganic species. These features open perspectives for the design of hybrid layered materials.
## 2.4. Characteristics of the OSIQ-induced QSFs
The formation of QSFs is related to the three structural characteristics of Q[n]s, namely the electrostatic potential negative portal carbonyl groups, the electrostatic potential neutral rigid hydrophobic cavity and the electrostatic potential positive outer surface. According to the interaction modes, QSFs could be roughly divided into three categories based on: i) coordination of the portal carbonyl oxygens of Q[n]s and the metal ions;[54-60] ii) inclusion of certain guest molecules within the cavities of Q[n]s;[26,61-66,122,123] and iii) the outer surface interaction of Q[n]s.[71- 73,82] Thus, QSFs as shown in Figure 2 could be further extend to those shown in Figure 46.
## 2.4.1  Simplicity of the OSIQ-induced QSFs
Among the three typical QSFs, it was obvious that those assembled through the OSIQ-induced QSFs were simple and feasible. For example, the most simplest being that the Q[n] was dissolved in a solvent, such as pure water or an acidic solutions, and then its solid crystals or microcrystals could then be collected. Because the components are comprised of only the $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{n}]$ used and solvent molecules, the mother liquor could be recycled. In addition, the cavities of the $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{n}]$ molecules or the channels or pores constructed from the $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{\eta}]$ molecules were be vacated upon the removal of solvent molecules after the activation of the OSIQinduced QSFs by vacuuming to a constant weight at $60-70^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . Thus, for practical applications, the channels, pores, and cavities could all be used. This endowed OSIQ-induced QSFs with more specific properties than those of other types of $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{n}]$ -based frameworks. Thus, simplicity was one of the characteristics of the OSIQ-induced QSFs.
## 2.4.2  Diversity of the OSIQ-induced QSFs
Because the OSIQ-induced QSFs are constructed through OSIQs, the area of interaction is broader than that of other $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{\eta}].$ - based frameworks, which increases their diversity. Firstly, this diversity is reflected in the fact that the same Q[n] in different media can form different OSIQ-induced QSFs. For example, Q[10] molecules form a OSIQ-induced QSF with triangular regions in aqueous HCl solutions (Figure 11b);[82] whereas in an aqueous $H N O_{3}$ solution, a Q[10]-based QSF was assembled through the OSIQ with square regions (Figure 11e).[82] Another example is that Q[8] molecules form the most common QSF in a compact stacking fashion in aqueous HCl solutions (Figure 47a),[80,124,125] two Q[8]-based QSFs with honeycomb-like structures in formic acid medium (Figure 47b),[73] and in an aqueous $H N O_{3}$ solution (Figure 47c),[80] respectively. The selfinduced OSIQ is the most important driving force.
This diversity is also reflected in the ways in which the OSIQinduced QSFs were constructed. As we have described, the OSIQ-induced QSFs were constructed by self-induction, anioninduction, or aromatic-induction. For the same Q[n], QSFs with different structures could be formed by employing different synthetic methods. In fact, we have obtained different Q[10]- based QSFs from hydrochloric acid (Figure 11b) or nitric acid (Figure 11e). The two acids contained different anions, $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ or ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}{}^{-}$ ,[82] as well as by introducing $\mathsf{C d C l}_{2}$ in aqueous HCl solution (4M) (Figure 30b)[106] and (8M) (Figure 31a),[107] respectively. We can also take Q[6] as an example to summarize the Q[6]-based QSFs assembled through the OSIQs obtained by different construction methods: First, the honeycomb-like Q[6]-based QSF assembled via the self-induced OSIQ in $2.4~\mathsf{M}$ hydrochloric acid solution was reported by Kim and co-workers (Figure 48a).[71-73] Secondly, Sun and co-workers also reported a honeycomb-like Q[6]-based QSF assembled via the aromatic-induced OSIQ in the presence of 4,4’,4’’-benzene-1,3,5-tribenzoic acid $(H_{3}B T B)$ , which acts as a structure directing agent (Figure 38a or Figure 48b).[115] Our group reported the Q[6]-based QSF assembled via the anioninduced OSIQ in the presence of $[P t C l_{6}]^{2-}$ as a structure directing agent (Figure 18b or Figure 48c).[96]
The diversity of the OSIQ-induced QSFs also originates from the many members of the $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{\eta}]$ family. On the one hand, the family members could be listed from the smallest Q[4] analogues[29,30] to the largest twisted Q[15].[31] Moreover, the family members also range from unsubstituted Q[n]s,[32-36] Q[n] analogues, Q[n] derivatives,[37-40] various alkyl-substituted Q[n]s,[41-53] and fully and partially hydroxylated Q[n]s and semi-Q[n]s[28] These various species could be used to construct numerous OSIQ-induced QSFs in different media and by employing different synthetic methods, the study of the OSIQ-induced QSFs and their functional properties could be an expansive research subject.
## 2.4.3  Modulability of the OSIQ-induced QSFs
For porous frameworks, an adjustable pore or channel size is generally required. In fact, when we introduced the three types of OSIQ-induced QSFs, they were all conducted according to the order of the size of the Q[n]s. For example, the anion-induced QSFs were from Q[5] to tQ[14], and different sizes of channels or pores were observed in the different size Q[n]-based supramolecular frameworks. Figure 49 summarized some of the typical QSFs cases. The regulation of pore or channel sizes in the QSFs was easily achieved by selecting Q[n]s of different sizes as building blocks. For example, the $[P t C l_{6}]^{2-}$ anion has been introduced as a structure directing agent in the Q[5]- and Q[6]-HCl systems, whereupon the obtained polar pore cross-sectional areas in the Q[n]-based supramolecular frameworks (Figures 49a,b) were about 58 Å2,[94] and $72\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{2}$ , respectively.[96] By introducing [CdCl4]2− or $[Z\mathsf{n C l}_{4}]^{2-}$ into Q[8]- and Q[10]-HCl systems, the pore cross-sectional areas in the obtained $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{\eta}].$ - based supramolecular frameworks (Figures 49c,d) were about 83 Å2 [88] and $173\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{2}$ ,[108] respectively.
If the channel was restricted by the $\mathsf{Q}[\boldsymbol{n}]$ molecules, its crosssectional area can be adjusted by using different Q[n]s, as shown in Figure 30b or Figure 50a.[106] The channels in the Q[10]-based supramolecular framework were restricted by Q[10] molecules. As reported by Bardelang and others, the pore sizes of the Q[8]-, Q[7]- and Q[6]-based supramolecular frameworks were restricted by Q[8],[126] Q[7][104] and Q[6],[126] respectively (Figures 50b-d). Moreover, Q[n]s were generally rigid and chemically stable, and these properties were maintained in their corresponding OSIQinduced QSFs.
