## 3. Summary and Outlook
In this review, we have summarized research on the OSIQ driven QSFs in $\mathsf Q[\boldsymbol{n}]$ -chemistry. According to the different species interacting with the outer surface of Q[n]s, the interactions for constructing QSFs could be roughly divided into three types: i) self-induced; ii) anion-induced; and iii) aromatic-induced:
i) For self-induced QSFs, the construction of QSFs is attributed to the dipole interactions formed between the electrostatic potential negative portal carbonyl oxygen atoms and the electrostatic potential positive outer surface of Q[n]s, including the interactions between portal carbonyl oxygen atoms of a $\mathsf Q[\boldsymbol{n}]$ molecule and the waist methine units, bridging methylene units, portal carbonyl carbon atoms of an adjacent Q[n] molecule, leading to various QSFs.
ii) For anion-induced QSF, the construction of the QSFs is attributed to the ion-dipole interactions between the electronegative anion and the electrostatic potential positive outer of the surface of the Q[n]s. The inorganic anions include various inorganic acidic ions, polychlorinated transition metal anions, HPA or POM anions. In the construction of QSFs, inorganic anions generally did not participate in the coordination of Q[n]s and inclusion therein.
iii) For aromatic-induced QSFs, the construction of the QSFs is attributed to the π-interactions of aromatic rings in the structure directing agent with the carbonyl groups of the pentacyclic rings of the Q[n]s, C-Hπ interactions of aromatic rings in the structure directing agent with the bridging methylene or waist methine units of Q[n]s. If the structure directing agent contained carboxylic anions, which interact with the electrostatic potential positive outer surfaces of adjacent $\mathsf Q[\boldsymbol{n}]$ molecules to form various QSFs.
It should be noted that the interaction of latticed water molecules with the portal carbonyl oxygen atoms always exist and play an important role in construction of OSIQ-induced QSFs.
When compared with other Q[n]-based frameworks, such as Q[n]-based metal-organic frameworks based on the coordination between Q[n] molecules and metal ions, guest@Q[n]-based supramolecular organic frameworks based on the interaction between Q[n] molecules and guests, and Q[n]-based frameworks based on the coordination between Q[n]-based pseudorotaxane and metal ions, OSIQ-induced QSFs are characterized by their simple composition, ease of synthesis, and high synthetic yield. In addition, the OSIQ-induced QSFs are amenable to diversification due to the various synthesis methods and the numerous Q[n]s and analogues available. Furthermore, by introducing Q[n]s of different sizes, the regulation of the OSIQinduced QSFs has been achieved. Finally, the OSIQ-induced QSFs can leave rooms for the co-inclusion during crystal growth, or be used in future target applications. Investigations on functional properties have shown that QSFs were outstanding for gas adsorption, selective metal ion sequestration, dye molecule adsorption, solid phase microextraction fiber coating adsorbent materials, and a host of other applications. These interesting compounds are expected to find wide applications in nanotechnology, molecular sieves, sensors, gas adsorption and separation, ion or molecular transport and heterogeneous catalysis.
